<BaseLayout title="What is UXO? | UXO.INFO" description="A plain-language introduction to unexploded ordnance.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Foundations</p><h1>What is unexploded ordnance?</h1><p class="lead">UXO is explosive ordnance that has been fired, dropped, launched, or otherwise deployed but did not function as intended and remains hazardous.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container split"><div><h2>One part of a wider problem.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>UXO is commonly discussed alongside other explosive remnants of war. The terms matter: they help distinguish a munition that failed to detonate from other abandoned or hazardous explosive items.</p><p>Whatever the category, the essential safety message is simple. Do not touch, move, investigate, or attempt to make an item safe. Leave the area when possible and report it through appropriate local channels.</p><p class="notice">This site provides general awareness information only. Follow local authority and specialist advice in an actual situation.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section section--quiet"><div class="container split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Read further</p><h2>Use primary references.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>The ICRC explains UXO and other explosive remnants of war in plain language, while the legal framework for recording, warnings, risk education, marking, and clearance is set out in Protocol V to the Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons.</p><a class="action-link" href="/resources">Sources &amp; resources</a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
